WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:04.254
In 2012, a Canadian mining company 
sent a ship out

00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:07.382
to explore this remote area 
of the Pacific Ocean.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:09.926
A few hundred miles 
southeast of Hawaii--

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.637
It's called the Clarion-Clipperton Zone.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.223
Lying deep on its sea floor

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:17.142
thousands of meters below the surface 

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:21.312
is a treasure trove of metals and minerals 
worth billions of dollars.

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:23.398
We need these for everything. 

00:00:23.398 --> 00:00:25.442
Like electrical wiring, 
stainless steel

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:28.653
car engines, jet engines, 
computers and phones.

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:31.573
Land-based deposits of these minerals 

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:33.491
around the world have met our needs so far...

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.036
But our needs are changing.

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:39.122
To fully decarbonize, 
we need clean energy.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:41.082
And that requires more metals...

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:44.044
up to six times more 
to get us there.

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:47.839
Estimates show that there are 
more of these metals

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:49.424
in the Clarion-Clipperton Zone...

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.343
than all these land-based deposits combined.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:55.013
That's why the Canadian company is not alone.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.141
Today, there are 16 other exploration ships

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:00.810
representing various countries and 
private companies...

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.729
that are in a race to the seafloor. 

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:07.275
Which one of them becomes the first 
to mine the deep sea

00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:10.070
will depend on an obscure 
UN organization

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.822
that's currently faced with 
two critical questions:

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.576
Is the fight against climate change worth

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.454
the irreversible environmental damage 
of seabed mining?

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.915
And should a few nations

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.292
get to profit off a shared natural resource...

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:26.586
just because they were 
the first to get there?

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:39.015
The UN began establishing 
the laws of the sea in 1958.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.850
They eventually decided

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:44.938
 that 12 miles off a coastal country shores 
is their sovereignty territory.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.733
200 miles after that is their 
exclusive economic zone...

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.527
where the country can have fisheries

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:53.321
or drill for oil and gas...
or mine.

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:57.200
But beyond that, about 72% 
of the deep ocean

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.036
sits outside the jurisdiction 
of any one country.

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:02.455
They simply call it The Area. 

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:06.042
And it became the common heritage 
of all mankind.

00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:08.419
That’s where the Clarion-Clipperton Zone...

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.297
with all its mineral riches 
is located.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.803
We've known about the metallic bounty here 
since the 1870s...

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:19.013
when the British HMS Challenger

00:02:19.013 --> 00:02:21.724
made a journey around the world
to survey the ocean.

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:23.518
While over the Pacific...

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:27.021
explorers wrote of several peculiar 
black oval bodies

00:02:27.021 --> 00:02:28.940
they had dredged up from the seafloor.

00:02:29.566 --> 00:02:33.069
These were rocks roughly the size of apples 
packed with metals

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:35.113
like manganese, cobalt, 
and nickel.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.116
Oceanographers would go on 
to discover metals

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:39.534
throughout the world's seafloor...

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.828
in three different types of deposits.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.414
They are in hydrothermal vents

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:46.207
which are like underwater hot springs.

00:02:46.541 --> 00:02:49.836
Encrusted in the slopes and summits 
of undersea mountains.

00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:51.754
And found in the form of rocks

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:54.549
lying on vast, flat plains 
on the seafloor.

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:57.010
The most abundant collection of these

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.679
are at the bottom of the Clarion-Clipperton zone.

00:03:01.222 --> 00:03:02.932
These discoveries sparked the interest

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:06.269
of multinational companies 
in the 1960s and 70s...

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:08.771
who were gearing up to mine 
the ocean floor

00:03:08.771 --> 00:03:11.524
for industrial uses like electrical wiring

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.359
stainless steel and fertilizer.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:15.778
Companies from China, Japan

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:19.324
the Soviet Union, Australia, the US, 
and European countries...

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.618
descended on the Clarion-Clipperton zone.

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:23.369
They tested mining equipment

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.372
and took some of the first photos
of the rocks on the seafloor.

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:30.168
It was during this early chaos at sea

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:32.378
that the Maltese delegate to the UN

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.839
made a pivotal speech warning against

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:37.091
repeating the mistakes of colonialism.

00:03:37.675 --> 00:03:39.552
He said that the race would reserve

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.722
the plurality of the world's resources
for the exclusive benefit

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.016
of less than a handful 
of nations.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:48.436
The strong would get stronger, 
the rich richer.

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.565
“All those in favor, please press
the green button.”

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.819
So in 1982, the UN met to adopt 
additional laws of the sea...

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.697
and it was signed by over 100 countries...

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.368
with three main conditions 
for mining in the area.

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:07.997
It must benefit all of humankind, 
irrespective of location.

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:11.501
Consider the special interests and needs 
of developing countries.

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:14.921
And ensure protection of 
the marine environment.

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:16.839
To enforce these new rules

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.217
they established the International Seabed Authority...

00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:21.719
or ISA, in Kingston, Jamaica.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.222
Every country that signed 
the laws of the sea

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:25.974
would be a member state of the ISA.

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:30.687
Today, that number is up to 168, 
plus the European Union.

00:04:31.187 --> 00:04:34.732
Of these, 36 countries are voted in 
every few years

00:04:34.732 --> 00:04:37.110
to review applications for 
mining in the deep sea.

00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:40.029
Before a country can get permission to mine...

00:04:40.405 --> 00:04:42.615
it has to apply for an exploration contract

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:43.783
through this council.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.327
When the council approves an application...

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:51.124
it gives a 75,000 square kilometer portion
of the deep sea to the applicant.

00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:55.795
And to keep things fair, it sets aside 
a portion of equal value 

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.463
for a developing country to claim.

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:01.676
But other countries or companies 
can get access to this reserved area

00:05:01.676 --> 00:05:04.178
by partnering with developing countries.

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:08.057
That's how this ship ended up here in 2012.

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:12.854
The mining company called 
the Metals Company is based in Canada...

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:17.942
but they sought out a sponsor in the tiny 
Pacific Island nation of Nauru.

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:21.321
Together, they applied for an exploration contract...

00:05:21.738 --> 00:05:24.907
and the company got access 
to Nauru's reserved area.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:27.702
In 2015 and 2020...

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:30.246
the Metals Company got two more 
Pacific islands...

00:05:30.538 --> 00:05:33.791
Kiribati and Tonga to sponsor them, 
so they were able to claim 

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.544
even more areas reserved for developing countries.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:40.965
So far, they've been using their 
exploration contracts to test equipment...

00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:43.760
do environmental reviews and 
collect rock samples.

00:05:44.552 --> 00:05:46.721
Over the years, more and more applications

00:05:46.721 --> 00:05:48.639
have been approved to explore the area.

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:53.102
So far, the ISA has approved 
all 31 exploration applications

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:55.897
submitted by 22 different companies 
or countries...

00:05:56.481 --> 00:05:59.776
and 17 of them have been in 
the Clarion-Clipperton Zone.

00:06:01.819 --> 00:06:03.446
To access the rocks like the ones

00:06:03.446 --> 00:06:05.656
lying on the bottom of 
the Clarion-Clipperton zone...

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.202
mining companies use what looks like
 a large robotic vacuum

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:11.579
to sweep them up and pull them 
to the surface.

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:14.374
Proponents of deep sea mining 
argue that this method

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:16.751
has far less impact than 
land-based mining...

00:06:17.126 --> 00:06:20.213
like cobalt mining in Congo that 
contaminates waterways...

00:06:20.505 --> 00:06:23.257
and nickel mining in Indonesia 
that has deforested

00:06:23.257 --> 00:06:25.385
over a million acres of rainforest.

00:06:26.302 --> 00:06:28.679
But opponents believe 
agitating the seafloor

00:06:28.679 --> 00:06:32.016
at thousands of meters deep 
would destroy an ecosystem

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:33.893
we're still trying to understand.

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:36.396
The rocks in the Clarion-Clipperton zone

00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:38.439
took millions of years to form.

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:40.942
Unique creatures that live 
nowhere else on earth

00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:44.070
have adapted to live among them 
in this extreme environment

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.114
like types of sponges and mollusks

00:06:46.114 --> 00:06:47.990
that have built their habitats on the rocks.

00:06:48.825 --> 00:06:50.493
Thousands of new species are still

00:06:50.493 --> 00:06:52.745
being discovered today in 
the Clarion-Clipperton Zone...

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.164
like types of sea cucumber 
and starfish.

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:59.252
The fear is that mining in these 
dark and quiet ecosystems 

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.962
introduces noise and light...

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:03.756
and the machinery kicks up 
massive sediment plumes

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.884
that travel for miles underwater 
and deters marine life.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:10.930
In a 2020 study of a mining test 
off the coast of Japan

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.266
researchers found that digging up 
their seabed caused

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:18.187
as much as a 43% decline in fish 
and shrimp populations for up to a year

00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:19.856
after a sediment plume was triggered.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:22.233
Similar plumes are expected

00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:24.068
from mining in the Clarion-Clipperton Zone.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:26.737
This is why at least 21 countries

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:29.949
have called for a moratorium 
or a ban on deep sea mining.

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:33.703
And why these exploration crews 
preparing to mine

00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:36.080
are drawing protests from 
the environmental community.

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:42.253
Despite this opposition, in 2021

00:07:42.545 --> 00:07:45.047
The Metals Company's CEO 
rang the opening bell

00:07:45.047 --> 00:07:46.632
at the New York Stock Exchange.

00:07:46.924 --> 00:07:48.801
"The future is metallic."

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:52.263
The company had gone public...

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.891
a major step towards commercial mining.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:57.477
That same year

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:01.189
The Metals Company and Nauru 
sent a notice to the ISA council

00:08:01.189 --> 00:08:03.399
saying they planned to apply to mine.

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:07.487
But the thing is, beyond 
these broad ideals 

00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:11.073
that ISA hasn't set specific mining regulations 
for the deep seafloor.

00:08:11.532 --> 00:08:14.035
So this notice triggered a two-year rule...

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:18.247
which set a deadline for the ISA 
to figure out regulations.

00:08:19.123 --> 00:08:20.833
In July of 2023...

00:08:21.042 --> 00:08:23.336
the ISA met with that goal in mind.

00:08:24.587 --> 00:08:29.467
ISA countries in support of deep sea mining 
like China, Norway, and the UK...

00:08:29.717 --> 00:08:33.387
argue that our clean energy transition 
depends on finding more metals...

00:08:34.597 --> 00:08:36.849
and The Metals Company says 
this is their mission:

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:40.561
"to help the project of decarbonizing 
global energy and transport."

00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:45.066
But countries opposed argue that 
we just don't know enough

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:48.653
about the deep sea to risk the irreversible damage
it would cause.

00:08:48.986 --> 00:08:51.822
And that the ISA is still very far 
from figuring out 

00:08:51.822 --> 00:08:53.950
a fair way to share the profits from mining.

00:08:54.951 --> 00:08:56.869
So they reached a stalemate.

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:00.289
No regulations have been issued for mining.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.335
The Metals Company has announced

00:09:04.335 --> 00:09:07.630
that they will submit their mining application in 2024...

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:10.091
which has reset the clock for the ISA

00:09:10.091 --> 00:09:12.218
to come to a consensus on regulations.

00:09:12.802 --> 00:09:15.721
That means The Metals Company 
may soon win this race

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:17.181
the bottom of the ocean...

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:19.016
and others would follow.

00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:22.603
It will be difficult to meet this 
growing need for metals

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:25.147
without tapping the resources 
of the deep sea.

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:27.108
But by going in...

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.695
we could just end up destroying 
both the land and the sea

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:31.654
for more metals.

00:09:33.155 --> 00:09:35.241
What can mitigate the effects of this race

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:37.076
is a different kind of race.

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:39.996
One that’s been running parallel 
to the pursuit of mining.

00:09:40.788 --> 00:09:42.999
Research ships have been rushing to document

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:46.085
the ecosystem at the bottom 
of the Clarion-Clipperton Zone.

00:09:46.419 --> 00:09:49.839
What they find could minimize the damage
we are about to inflict...

00:09:51.215 --> 00:09:53.676
or at least we'll know what we're about to lose...

00:09:53.801 --> 00:09:55.970
in order to solve our climate crisis.

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:00.349
If you're still here:

00:10:00.349 --> 00:10:03.477
Hello, and thank you so much
for watching this video we produced

00:10:03.853 --> 00:10:06.147
Before you go, we have a request for you.

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:09.817
A series like Atlas takes so much 
time and care to produce.

00:10:10.192 --> 00:10:12.278
I don't make Atlas videos very often

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:15.406
but when I do, it's such a reminder 
what a huge lift

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:18.159
every step of the process is 
from the reporting...

00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:23.289
to the animation to fact checking every 
country border we show on-screen.

00:10:23.331 --> 00:10:26.626
Map explainers are a huge part of our channel's DNA

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:29.337
and we want to keep making them, 
which is where you come in.

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:32.423
In case you didn't know, our videos 
are part of a larger company

00:10:32.423 --> 00:10:35.384
that does great writing and 
podcast production too.

00:10:35.384 --> 00:10:37.637
and we are all determined 
to keep our work free

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:39.639
and not hide it behind a paywall.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.141
Which is why we're turning to you 
to support our work

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:43.434
through contributions.

00:10:43.434 --> 00:10:47.188
If you've enjoyed, learned from,
been entertained

00:10:47.188 --> 00:10:50.107
by our videos and are in a position
